void modifyVector(std::vector<std::shared_ptr<int>>& vec, int val, size_t threshold) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < vec.size() && i <= threshold; ++i) {
        if (vec[i] && *vec[i] > val) {
            // Replace with a new shared pointer containing twice the value of given integer
            vec[i].reset(new int(2*val));
            return;
        }
    }

    if (vec.size() <= threshold) {
        // Append a new shared pointer with doubled value to end of vector
        vec.push_back(std::make_shared<int>(2*val));
    }
}
```
This function efficiently traverses the vector using a for loop, checking each element and comparing it to the given integer. If an element is found that is greater than the integer, it replaces that shared pointer with a new one containing twice the value of the integer. 

If no such elements are found within the performance threshold, and if appending another element to the vector would not exceed this limit, then it adds a new shared pointer to the end of the vector, also containing twice the given integer.

The function uses smart pointers (`std::shared_ptr`) for memory management, ensuring that when a shared pointer is no longer in use, its memory will be automatically deallocated. The function also checks if the number of operations performed exceeds the performance threshold before proceeding to avoid overloading the system and maintain responsiveness under high load.
']

