['Here is an example of how you might design this module in C#. The example uses the `System.Data.SqlClient` namespace for database access, but similar concepts can be applied with other .NET RDBMS providers.

```Csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Data;
using System.Data.SqlClient;

public class BatchProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly SqlConnection _connection;
    private readonly int _batchSize;

    public BatchProcessor(string connectionString, int batchSize)
    {
        _connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString);
        _batchSize = batchSize;
        _connection.Open();
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<IDataReader>> ExecuteQueriesAsync(List<string> queries)
    {
        var results = new List<Task<SqlDataReader>>();

        for (var i = 0; i < queries.Count; i += _batchSize)
        {
            var batch = queries.GetRange(i, Math.Min(_batchSize, queries.Count - i));

            results.Add(Task.Run(() => ExecuteBatchAsync(batch)));
        }

        return await Task.WhenAll(results);
    }

    private async Task<SqlDataReader> ExecuteBatchAsync(List<string> batch)
    {
        using (var transaction = _connection.BeginTransaction())
        {
            try
            {
                var command = new SqlCommand();
                command.Connection = _connection;
                command.Transaction = transaction;

                foreach (var query in batch)
                {
                    command.CommandText = query;
                    await command.ExecuteReaderAsync(CommandBehavior.CloseConnection);
                }

                transaction.Commit();
            }
            catch (Exception ex) when (ex is SqlException || ex is TimeoutException)
            {
                // Handle transient errors and timeouts
                Console.WriteLine($"Error executing query: {ex.Message}");
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _connection?.Dispose();
    }
}
```
In this code, `BatchProcessor` encapsulates the logic for executing a list of SQL queries in batches using the provided connection string and batch size. It leverages async/await for non-blocking execution and parallel processing of the batches to optimize resource utilization. Connection pooling is managed automatically by the .NET framework, but you can further fine-tune this using the connection string parameters (like `Max Pool Size`).

This example does not include all your requirements like query optimization, error handling with retry, dynamic execution plans, integration with existing systems and so on. You will need to add these features yourself based on your specific needs and constraints. 

Also note that this is just a basic skeleton and doesn't handle many edge cases. For instance, it assumes all queries are read-only, don't require transactions across multiple tables, etc. In a real-world application you should implement error handling and transaction management in more detail to meet your specific requirements. 

Lastly, always remember to validate and sanitize input data before using them in queries to avoid SQL injection attacks. It's also recommended to use parameterized queries or ORM frameworks to protect against such issues.
