['Here's a simple sample Java program for managing continuous stream of data from multiple external sources using threads. This is just an example, actual implementation would be complex with consideration to many factors such as error handling, configuration management etc.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.Consumer;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.exception.ExceptionUtils;

public class StreamDataService {
    private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
    private BlockingQueue<Object> buffer = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    private boolean isPaused = false;
    private CountDownLatch paused = new CountDownLatch(1);
    private CountDownLatch resumed = new CountDownLatch(1);

    // Assume MessagingSystem and PersistentStorage are two external classes.
    private MessagingSystem messagingSystem;
    private PersistentStorage persistentStorage;

    public StreamDataService() {
        this.messagingSystem = new MessagingSystem();
        this.persistentStorage = new PersistentStorage();

        // Start processing data in a separate thread.
        executor.submit(this::processData);
        messagingSystem.sendNotification("Started");
    }

    public void processData() {
        while (true) {
            try {
                if (!isPaused && buffer.size() > 0) {
                    Object data = buffer.take();
                    // Process your data here.

                    // Store processed data to persistent storage.
                    persistentStorage.store(data);
                } else if (isPaused){
                    paused.await();  // Will block until isPaused set to false.
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                System.out.println("Data processing thread interrupted");
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public void addData(Object data) {
        buffer.add(data);

        // If paused, unpause to start consuming buffered data.
        if (isPaused && buffer.size() > 0) {
            isPaused = false;
            resumed.countDown();  // Unblock the consumer thread.
        }
    }

    public void pauseService() {
        isPaused = true;

        messagingSystem.sendNotification("Paused");
    }

    public void resumeService() {
        if (isPaused) {
            isPaused = false;
            resumed.countDown();  // Unblock the consumer thread.

            messagingSystem.sendNotification("Resumed");
        }
    }

    public void stopService() {
        try {
            // Flush any remaining buffered data to persistent storage before shutting down.
            while (buffer.size() > 0) {
                Object data = buffer.take();
                persistentStorage.store(data);
            }

            messagingSystem.sendNotification("Stopped");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exceptions and notify about the failure to flush the buffered data.
            System.out.println("Failed to flush remaining data: " + ExceptionUtils.getStackTrace(e));
        } finally {
            executor.shutdown();  // Shut down processing thread pool.
        }
    }
}
```

This is a simple example and does not cover all the requirements of your problem statement, such as handling varying data formats and rates efficiently, integrating with different messaging systems or persistent storage solutions etc., but it gives you an idea how to create such service in Java. You can enhance this code according to your needs by adding appropriate error handling, logging mechanisms, configuration settings etc.
']

["Here is a possible solution in JavaScript using an iterative approach and creating a Priority Queue for sorting and merging all arrays together. 
```javascript
// Create priority queue class
class PriorityQueue {
    constructor(comparator) {
        this._heap = [];
        this._comparator = comparator;
    }

    size() {
        return this._heap.length;
    }

    isEmpty() {
        return this.size() == 0;
    }

    peek() {
        return this._heap[0];
    }

    push(value) {
        this._heap.push(value);
        this._siftUp();
        return this.size();
    }

    pop() {
        const poppedValue = this.peek();
        const bottom = this.size() - 1;
        if (bottom > 0) {
            this._swap(0, bottom);
        }
        this._heap.pop();
        this._siftDown();
        return poppedValue;
    }

    replace(value) {
        const replacedValue = this.peek();
        this._heap[0] = value;
        this._siftDown();
        return replacedValue;
    }

    _parent(index) {
        return Math.floor((index - 1) / 2);
    }

    _leftChild(index) {
        return index * 2 + 1;
    }

    _rightChild(index) {
        return index * 2 + 2;
    }

    _greater(i, j) {
        return this._comparator(this._heap[i], this._heap[j]);
    }

    _swap(i, j) {
        [this._heap[i], this._heap[j]] = [this._heap[j], this._heap[i]];
    }

    _siftUp() {
        let nodeIndex = this.size() - 1;
        while (nodeIndex > 0 && this._greater(nodeIndex, this._parent(nodeIndex))) {
            this._swap(nodeIndex, this._parent(nodeIndex));
            nodeIndex = this._parent(nodeIndex);
        }
    }

    _siftDown() {
        let nodeIndex = 0;
        while (
            (this._leftChild(nodeIndex) < this.size() && this._greater(this._leftChild(nodeIndex), nodeIndex)) ||
            (this._rightChild(nodeIndex) < this.size() && this._greater(this._rightChild(nodeIndex), nodeIndex))
        ) {
            let greaterChild = (this._rightChild(nodeIndex) < this.size() && this._greater(this._rightChild(nodeIndex), this._leftChild(nodeIndex))) ? 
                this._rightChild(nodeIndex) : this._leftChild(nodeIndex);
            this._swap(greaterChild, nodeIndex);
            nodeIndex = greaterChild;
        }
    }
}

// Function to merge sorted arrays using priority queue
function mergeSortedArrays(arrays, comparatorFunction) {
    const mergedArray = []; // Store final result
    let lastPoppedValue = null; 

    // Create a priority queue with the comparator function for sorting arrays
    const priorityQueue = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => comparatorFunction(b[0], a[0]));

    // Push first element of each array into the queue
    arrays.forEach(array => {
        if (array.length > 0) 
            priorityQueue.push(array);
    });

    while (!priorityQueue.isEmpty()) {
        let currentArray = priorityQueue.pop(); // Get largest array from priority queue

        // Duplicates are handled by comparing with the last popped value
        if (lastPoppedValue !== currentArray[0]) { 
            mergedArray.push(currentArray[0]);
            lastPoppedValue = currentArray[0];
        }

        // Push new element of this array into queue if it's not empty
        currentArray.shift();
        if (currentArray.length > 0) 
            priorityQueue.push(currentArray);
    }

    return mergedArray;
}
```
Please note, the usage and configuration of `mergeSortedArrays` function requires external configurations which are not included in this code. You may need to adjust it according to your specific needs. 

Remember that JavaScript does not perform well with large recursive calls due to its single-threaded nature and its weak support for tail call optimization, so the iterative approach is more efficient and will not cause a stack overflow error. The tradeoff here is memory usage because of additional data structures such as priority queue. 

The function should efficiently handle edge cases where input arrays are empty or contain only identical elements by simply skipping them when pushing into the heap. It also minimizes memory usage with its focus on popping from and pushing back to the heap, ensuring that no duplicates exist in the merged array. 

However, this solution might not perform well under high concurrency scenarios since JavaScript is single-threaded and it's hard to handle multiple requests effectively. A more powerful language like Go or C++ may be better suited for these types of CPU intensive tasks. However, for most typical web applications where memory usage isn’t a huge concern, this solution should perform adequately.
"]

["Here's an outline of how you can approach writing such a JavaScript utility:

```javascript
class DynamicPageProcessor {
  constructor(selectorLogic = {}, transformFuncs = {}, apiClient) {
    this.selectorLogic = selectorLogic;
    this.transformFuncs = transformFuncs;
    this.apiClient = apiClient;
    this.metadataCache = new Map(); // Cache for metadata from the external API
  }

  async updateElement(element) {
    const selectors = Object.keys(this.selectorLogic).find(selector => element.matches(selector));

    if (selectors) {
      const selectorData = this.selectorLogic[selectors];

      // Fetch metadata asynchronously from the external API with a cache layer
      let metadata;
      if (this.metadataCache.has(element)) {
        metadata = this.metadataCache.get(element);
      } else {
        metadata = await this.apiClient.fetchMetadata(selectorData.metaUrl);
        this.metadataCache.set(element, metadata); // Cache the fetched metadata
      }

      const transformFunc = this.transformFuncs[selectorData.transformation];

      if (typeof transformFunc === 'function') {
        transformFunc(element, metadata);
      }
    }
  }

  observeDOMChanges() {
    // Use MutationObserver to handle real-time changes in the DOM
    const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
      mutations.forEach((mutation) => {
        if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
          mutation.addedNodes.forEach(node => this.updateElement(node));
        } else if (mutation.type === 'attributes' || mutation.type === 'characterData') {
          this.updateElement(mutation.target);
        }
      });
    });

    observer.observe(document, { childList: true, subtree: true, attributes: true, characterData: true });
  }
}
```
In the code above, we have created a class `DynamicPageProcessor` that takes in selector logic and transformation functions as dictionaries with selectors as keys. When an update occurs on the DOM, `MutationObserver` is used to listen for these changes and handle them accordingly. Each added or updated node is then processed by calling the `updateElement` function which determines what transformation should be applied based on its selector.

Please note that this is a simplified version of how such a utility might be designed. In a real-world scenario, you would need to handle potential edge cases and performance bottlenecks like inconsistent DOM structure or partially loaded content, manage network delays and asynchronous API calls, among others, according to the given constraints and trade-offs. For instance, caching strategies should be carefully chosen based on data freshness requirements and application's performance and resource usage constraints. 

Also, keep in mind that handling concurrent queries and managing multi-threaded environments is typically out of scope for JavaScript (it runs single-threaded). But you could use Web Workers to perform background processing if needed. Also consider using libraries like RxJS for reactive programming when dealing with large-scale datasets or complex data flow scenarios.
"]

["Below is an example code in Node.js that demonstrates how you could tackle this problem using dynamic module loading with dependency management, file naming generation using timestamp, and error handling/logging for each load event.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const crypto = require('crypto');
const events = require('events');
const EventEmitter = new events.EventEmitter();

// Generate unique identifiers with timestamp and a random number
function generateUniqueID() {
    const now = new Date();
    const timestamp = now.getTime();
    const randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100);

    return `${timestamp}-${randomNumber}`;
}

// Load and execute multiple JavaScript files from a specified directory
function loadAndExecuteFilesFromDirectory(directoryPath, extension) {
    fs.readdir(directoryPath, (err, files) => {
        if (err) {
            EventEmitter.emit('error', err);
            return;
        }

        const fileNames = files.filter((file) => path.extname(file) === extension).map((file) => path.join(directoryPath, file));

        loadFilesInOrder(fileNames, new Map());
    });
}

// Load and execute JavaScript files in order while checking for circular dependencies and missing dependencies
function loadFilesInOrder(filesToLoad, loadedModulesMap, loaded = []) {
    if (filesToLoad.length === 0) return;

    const filePath = filesToLoad[0];
    const uniqueID = generateUniqueID();

    try {
        // Check for circular dependencies
        if(loaded.includes(filePath)) throw new Error('Circular dependency detected');

        loadedModulesMap.set(uniqueID, require(filePath));
        loaded.push(filePath);

        EventEmitter.emit('load', { id: uniqueID, path: filePath });
    } catch (err) {
        EventEmitter.emit('error', err);
    } finally {
        filesToLoad = filesToLoad.slice(1);
        loadFilesInOrder(filesToLoad, loadedModulesMap, loaded);
    }
}
``` 
This script creates a function `loadAndExecuteFilesFromDirectory` that reads the directory specified by `directoryPath` and filters out files with extensions other than `extension`. It then calls another function `loadFilesInOrder` to load each file in order while checking for circular dependencies and missing dependencies. Error handling is performed using an event emitter which emits 'error' events whenever an error occurs during loading or execution, allowing you to handle these errors at a higher level of your application.
"]

