["Here is a basic implementation of such a function in C, using a linked list to manage unique RNTI values. This example uses `pthread` mutexes for synchronization. Note that this code does not include error checking and handling, which would be essential in a real-world scenario. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
    int CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int generatedRNTIs = 0;

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* generatePDUs(size_t count) {
    srand(time(NULL));
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *head = NULL, *current = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* newNode = malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
        if (!newNode) {
            // Handle error, e.g., return NULL and free all allocated memory
        }

        newNode->RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG = i;
        newNode->CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG = i;
        newNode->CRCFLAG = rand() % 2;
        ++generatedRNTIs;
        newNode->RNTI = generatedRNTIs;

        if (!head) {
            head = newNode;
            current = newNode;
        } else {
            current->next = newNode;
            current = newNode;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    return head;
}
```
This function generates a linked list of `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures, each with unique RNTI values. The mutex ensures that only one thread at a time can modify the `generatedRNTIs` variable, thus preventing race conditions. However, this solution does not integrate seamlessly into an existing system that manages a pool of `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structures and handles large-scale operations efficiently. This could require a more sophisticated approach or a dedicated memory management library. Also, it doesn't include error handling for dynamic memory allocation, which would be crucial in a real-world scenario.
