["This task can be quite complex due to the intricate requirements of OAuth 2.0 authentication and HTTP client management. Here is a basic implementation for this in PHP using GuzzleHttp, a popular HTTP client library for PHP. Note that security best practices should also be considered, such as using HTTPS and enforcing secure cookies over unsecured ones:

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';
use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use GuzzleHttp\Exception\RequestException;
use GuzzleHttp\Psr7\Response;

class OAuth2Service {
    private $client;
    private $accessToken;

    public function __construct($base_uri, $apiVersion) {
        $this->client = new Client(['base_uri' => $base_uri.'/v'.$apiVersion]);
        $this->sessionManager = new Session(); // This should be a third party session manager class.
    }

    public function authenticate($clientId, $clientSecret, $redirectUri) {
        try {
            $response = $this->client->post('/oauth/token', [
                'verify' => !APP_DEBUG, // Verify SSL certificate in production environment only.
                'form_params' => [
                    'grant_type' => 'client_credentials',
                    'client_id' => $clientId,
                    'client_secret' => $clientSecret,
                    'redirect_uri' => $redirectUri
                ]
            ]);
            $this->accessToken = json_decode($response->getBody())->access_token;
            $this->sessionManager->set('access_token', $this->accessToken);
        } catch (RequestException $e) {
            // Handle exceptions here.
        }
    }

    public function request($method, $endpoint, $headers = [], $queryParams = []) {
        $defaultHeaders = ['Authorization' => 'Bearer '.$this->accessToken];
        $mergedHeaders = array_merge($defaultHeaders, $headers);
        return new RequestBuilder(function() use ($method, $endpoint, $mergedHeaders, $queryParams) {
            try {
                return $this->client->$method($endpoint, [
                    'headers' => $mergedHeaders,
                    'query' => $queryParams
                ]);
            } catch (RequestException $e) {
                // Handle exceptions here.
            }
        });
    }
}

class RequestBuilder {
    private $callback;
    public function __construct($callback){
        $this->callback = $callback;
    }

    public function execute() {
        return call_user_func($this->callback);
    }
}
```
Please note that you may need to adjust this code according to your specific needs and third party libraries. This is a simplified version of the code, the actual implementation should handle different scenarios like handling refresh tokens for expired access tokens, rate limiting strategies, network timeouts etc. Also it's crucial to keep the OAuth credentials secure. 

