['Sure, below is a code block that meets your requirements using PHP as the language. Note that Union-Find data structure and caching mechanism are not implemented in this solution. The Union-Find data structure can be used for efficient merging of different subsets of users based on their common attributes. For caching mechanism, you could use Redis or Memcached libraries depending on your needs.

```php
class UserManagement {
    private $db; // PDO object to interact with the database
    private $cache; // Cache driver object to store and retrieve data quickly

    public function __construct($db, $cache) {
        $this->db = $db;
        $this->cache = $cache;
    }

    public function updateUserPassword($userId, $newPassword) {
        // Check password strength and handle errors
        $passwordStrength = self::validatePasswordStrength($newPassword);

        if (!$passwordStrength['success']) {
            return [
                'success' => false, 
                'message' => $passwordStrength['message']
            ];
        }

        // Check if user exists in cache first to reduce database load
        $user = $this->cache->get('user:' . $userId);

        if (!$user) {
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?");
            $stmt->execute([$userId]);
            $user = $stmt->fetch();

            // Cache user for future use
            $this->cache->set('user:' . $userId, $user);
        }

        if (!$user) {
            return ['success' => false, 'message' => 'User not found'];
        }

        $start = microtime(true); // Start timer to measure response time

        try {
            $stmt = $this->db->prepare("UPDATE users SET password = ? WHERE id = ?");
            $hashedPassword = password_hash($newPassword, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);

            if (!$stmt->execute([$hashedPassword, $userId])) {
                return ['success' => false, 'message' => 'Failed to update password'];
            }
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            return ['success' => false, 'message' => $e->getMessage()];
        }

        $end = microtime(true); // End timer

        return [
            'success' => true, 
            'message' => 'Password updated successfully',
            'response_time' => ($end - $start) * 1000 // Convert to milliseconds
        ];
    }

    private static function validatePasswordStrength($password) {
        if (strlen($password) < 8) {
            return ['success' => false, 'message' => 'Password must be at least 8 characters long'];
        }

        if (!preg_match('/[A-Za-z].*[0-9]|[0-9].*[A-Za-z]/', $password)) {
            return ['success' => false, 'message' => 'Password must contain at least one letter and one number'];
        }

        return ['success' => true];
    }
}
```
This code implements a user management class with methods to update user passwords. The strength of the password is validated before trying to update it in the database, and any error messages are returned if validation fails. A cache mechanism has been implemented to reduce load on the database by storing frequently accessed data. 

In terms of scaling up to a large user base, this solution could be further optimized by using techniques such as sharding (breaking up the dataset into smaller parts) or replication (copying the dataset across multiple servers). However, these solutions require significant architectural changes and are beyond the scope of a simple function. 

Asynchronous operations are not directly supported in PHP due to its single-threaded nature, but this can be overcome by using Swoole or ReactPHP libraries which allow for non-blocking I/O operations. This allows other operations to continue running while waiting for the database operation to complete, thus improving throughput under stress conditions.
']

