    while (temp != NULL && temp->data.RNTI != RNTI) {
        prev = temp;
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    if (temp == NULL) return; // No node with given RNTI

    // Unlink the node from list
    if (prev != NULL)
        prev->next = temp->next;

    // Change head
    if (head == temp)
        head = temp->next;

    free(temp); // Free memory

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This code implements a singly linked list and provides functions to add, update, or remove elements. The operations are protected by a mutex for thread safety. This is just an example, and you would need to consider more complex solutions for memory management, error handling, integration with other systems, scalability, usability, fault tolerance, etc. Also, keep in mind that C doesn't support garbage collection or automatic memory management like higher-level languages do, so manual memory management is a necessity when dealing with low-level data structures like linked lists.
