['Here's a simple implementation of `renderFooterScripts` in JavaScript:

```javascript
let processedArrays = {}; // Cache for storing already processed arrays

function renderFooterScripts(scripts) {
    if (!Array.isArray(scripts)) return '';  // Edge case: empty array or null/undefined input

    let html = "";
    let timestamp = new Date().toISOString();  // Track script processing time

    for (let script of scripts) {
        if (typeof script !== 'object' || !script.hasOwnProperty('scriptId') || !script.hasOwnProperty('scriptCode')) {
            console.error(`Invalid script object: ${JSON.stringify(script)}`);  // Data integrity issue: invalid or incomplete script object
            continue;
        }

        let id = script.scriptId;

        if (!processedArrays[id]) processedArrays[id] = 0;

        // Handle duplicate IDs by appending suffix _dupe and incrementing count
        if (++processedArrays[id] > 1) {  
            console.warn(`Duplicate scriptId: ${id}. Appending _dupe to maintain uniqueness.`);
            id += '_dupe';
        }

        // Optional metadata defaults
        let defer = (script.defer === undefined || typeof script.defer !== 'boolean') ? true : script.defer;  // Default: load scripts after HTML parsing is complete and execute them asynchronously 
        let async = (script.async === undefined || typeof script.async !== 'boolean') ? true : script.async;  // Default: load and execute scripts asynchronously without blocking rendering of remaining page content

        html += `<script id="${id}" defer=${defer} async=${async}>${script.scriptCode}</script>\n`;  // Render script as HTML string
    }

    return `<!-- Last processed: ${timestamp} -->\n${html}`;  // Include timestamp comment for tracking
}
```

This function checks if an array has already been processed by checking the cache. If it hasn't, it then processes each script in the array and handles duplicate IDs while appending a suffix to ensure uniqueness. It also sets default values for optional metadata and validates script objects. The rendered HTML string includes a timestamp comment to track when scripts were last processed.
']

["The following example code outlines the basic structure of such a module using Node.js. It uses streams and buffering to manage high-frequency event data, while utilizing Promises for flow control and error handling. This is a simplified version that doesn't include all your specifications (like integration with an existing log aggregation pipeline and separate analytics service), but it gives you a good idea of how to structure such a module:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const stream = require('stream');
let eventBuffer = [];
let paused = false;

class EventStream extends stream.Transform {
  constructor(options) {
    super(options);
  }

  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    if (!paused) {
      eventBuffer.push(chunk);

      // Flush data in configurable chunks to disk
      if (eventBuffer.length >= process.env.BUFFER_SIZE) {
        this._flushData();
      }
    } 

    callback();
  }

  _flush(callback) {
    if (!paused && eventBuffer.length > 0) {
      this._flushData();
    }

    callback();
  }

  pause() {
    paused = true;
  }

  resume() {
    paused = false;
  }

  _flushData() {
    const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      fs.appendFile('./data_log.txt', JSON.stringify(eventBuffer), 'utf8', (err) => {
        if (err) {
          console.error('Error flushing data to disk: ', err);
          reject();
        } else {
          eventBuffer = [];
          resolve();
        }
      }); 
    });

    promise.then(() => {
      // Continue processing
    })
    .catch(() => {
      // Handle error, possibly retry or escalate issue to developers
    });
  }
}

module.exports = EventStream;
```
The module starts by creating a new EventStream object that can receive incoming data as 'chunks'. It checks whether it's paused before processing the chunk, and if not, it buffers the data in memory and flushes it to disk when the buffer reaches a certain size (as set by an environment variable). This allows you to dynamically manage memory usage while ensuring minimal latency during peak traffic. 

This is a basic example and real-world implementation would require more sophisticated mechanisms for managing memory use, including potentially splitting buffers into smaller chunks based on the size of the data, dynamically adjusting buffer sizes according to system load, etc. It should also be integrated with an existing log aggregation pipeline and separate analytics service.
"]

