["Here is a simple implementation of such a JavaScript function using recursion to traverse the DOM tree:

```javascript
let visitedElements = {}; // Caching strategy for already visited elements
function traverseDOM(selector, testFunction) {
    if (typeof selector !== 'string' || typeof testFunction !== 'function') {
        console.error('Invalid input types');
        return;
    }

    const nodes = document.querySelectorAll(selector);

    for (let node of nodes) {
        if (!visitedElements[node]) { // Safeguard against cyclical DOM structures
            visitedElements[node] = true;
            testFunction(node);

            window.setTimeout(() => traverseDOM('.child', testFunction), 0); // Asynchronous handling
        }
    }
}
```
This function first checks the input types to ensure they're as expected, then it selects all elements that match the given selector string and applies the provided test function to each element. The visitedElements object is used as a caching strategy to avoid infinite loops in case of cyclical DOM structures. We use setTimeout with 0 delay to schedule the recursive call for next tick, ensuring the function doesn't block due to large amount of elements or ongoing async operations.
"]

