['Here is a basic example of such function. This code demonstrates loading and merging serialized objects from multiple binary files using the `BinaryFormatter` class in C#:

```Csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using System.Threading;
using System.Diagnostics;

public class ObjectMerger<T> where T : class
{
    private readonly ConcurrentBag<T> _bag = new ConcurrentBag<T>();
    private readonly object _lockObject = new object();
    private readonly BinaryFormatter _formatter = new BinaryFormatter();

    public void MergeFromFile(string filePath)
    {
        try
        {
            using (var stream = File.OpenRead(filePath))
            {
                lock (_lockObject)
                {
                    while (stream.Position < stream.Length)
                    {
                        var obj = _formatter.Deserialize(stream);
                        if (obj is T typedObj)
                            _bag.Add(typedObj);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Trace.WriteLine($"Error loading and merging from file: {filePath}. Error: {ex}");
        }
    }

    public T[] GetMergedObjects() => _bag.ToArray();
}
```
Please note that this code has some limitations such as using a global lock for concurrency, which could lead to performance issues with heavy load. For better concurrent access and more efficient handling of large files you might need a different approach using `FileStream` for reading the file in chunks rather than loading it entirely into memory at once, or using custom serialization format that supports chunk-by-chunk deserialization like Protocol Buffers. 

Also note that logging errors directly to the trace API (System.Diagnostics.Trace) is a simple approach but may not be suitable for production code, especially if you need to persist logs across different runs of your application or handle them in a more structured way. You might want to use a dedicated logging library instead for this purpose.
