['Here is a simple implementation of such a method using Java Serialization:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.SerializationUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(Deserializer.class);

    // LRU cache for deserialized objects
    private LinkedHashMap<String, Object> cache;
    private int maxCacheSize;

    public Deserializer(int maxCacheSize) {
        this.maxCacheSize = maxCacheSize;
        this.cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(this.maxCacheSize * 4 / 5, 0.7f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(java.util.Map.Entry<String, Object> eldest) {
                return size() > Deserializer.this.maxCacheSize;
            }
        };
    }

    public synchronized <T> T deserializeAndVerify(byte[] data, Class<T> clazz) {
        String key = new String(data);

        // Check cache first
        if (cache.containsKey(key)) {
            return clazz.cast(cache.get(key));
        }

        Object object;
        try {
            InputStream is = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
            object = SerializationUtils.deserialize(is);

            // Verify the integrity of the serialized data
            if (object.getClass() != clazz || data.length != SerializationUtils.serialize(object).length) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Serialized object size or class mismatch!");
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Deserialization error", e);
            throw new RuntimeException("Deserialization error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Add to cache if within limit
        if (cache.size() < maxCacheSize) {
            cache.put(key, object);
        }

        return clazz.cast(object);
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a simple example and doesn't account for all the mentioned factors and requirements. It also uses an external library (Apache Commons Lang) for serialization which might not be available in every environment, and it assumes that objects to be deserialized are Strings or other Serializable classes. Adjustments may be needed depending on your specific use case and environment. Also this does not log deserialization attempts as it's usually better done at a higher level (e.g., by wrapping the method call), but you can easily add that in the above code.
']

