    while (scanf("%d", &num) == 1){
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *data = malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T)); 

        if(!data){
            deleteList(&head); // Deallocating all memory if allocation fails
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Unlocking the mutex before returning
            return NULL;
        }

        data->RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG = rand() % 100;
        data->RNTI = num;
        data->CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG = rand() % 2;

        addItem(&head, data);
        arr[i++] = num;
    }

    printf("Max integer: %d\n", maxIntArray(arr, i));

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); // Unlocking the mutex before returning
}
```
This function will accept integers from stdin and add them to a linked list. Each node contains an NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structure with random values for RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG and CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG. The RNTI is set to the input integer.

The function also locks a mutex before entering a critical section, where it performs operations on shared data structures (linked list). This ensures thread-safety for high-load conditions by avoiding race conditions that could occur if multiple threads were manipulating the linked list simultaneously. After performing its tasks in the critical section, the function unlocks the mutex to allow other threads to access the linked list.

If memory allocation fails while adding items to the linked list, all allocated nodes are deallocated using deleteList and the function returns NULL. This is done to prevent memory leaks by ensuring all dynamically allocated resources are properly freed. After a failure, the mutex is also unlocked before returning to avoid deadlock situations where multiple threads wait indefinitely for access that will never be granted due to another thread holding the lock.
