["Here is a basic implementation of this task using C and Pthreads for thread safety. I'm using a simple hash table (in linked list format) for quick access to network interface data. However, it doesn't include logging service integration or memory optimization which you mentioned in your question. Also, please note that real world implementation would require more consideration for error handling and edge cases, this code is just a base to start from:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

// Network interface statistics structure
typedef struct net_stat {
    time_t timestamp;  // Timestamp of the statistic record
    char* interface_id;  // Interface identifier
    int* connections;  // Array of connection IDs
    int num_connections;  // Number of connection IDs
    struct net_stat* next;  // Next network interface in the hash table bucket
} NetStat;

// Hash table for network interfaces
#define NUM_BUCKETS 1024
NetStat** hashTable = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

// Initialize the hash table
void initHashTable() {
    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);
    hashTable = (NetStat**)calloc(NUM_BUCKETS, sizeof(NetStat*));
}

// Hash function for network interface identifier
unsigned long hashFunction(char* str) {
    unsigned long hash = 5381;
    int c;

    while ((c = *str++))
        hash = ((hash << 5) + hash) + c;  // hash * 33 + c

    return hash % NUM_BUCKETS;
}

// Add a new connection to the network interface statistics
void addConnection(char* interface_id, int conn_id) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Calculate the bucket index using hash function
    unsigned long bucket = hashFunction(interface_id);
    NetStat* current = NULL;

    // Check if network interface already exists in hash table
    for (current = hashTable[bucket]; current != NULL; current = current->next) {
        if (strcmp(current->interface_id, interface_id) == 0)
            break;
    }

    // If network interface does not exist in hash table, create a new one
    if (current == NULL) {
        current = (NetStat*)malloc(sizeof(NetStat));
        current->interface_id = strdup(interface_id);
        current->num_connections = 0;
        current->next = hashTable[bucket];
        hashTable[bucket] = current;
    }

    // Add new connection ID to the list and update timestamp
    time(&current->timestamp);
    current->connections = (int*)realloc(current->connections, sizeof(int) * (current->num_connections + 1));
    current->connections[current->num_connections++] = conn_id;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
For logging service integration and memory optimization, you need to integrate with the external service API or use a library that handles this, which depends on specifics of your project. Also, please note that I've used an external `time` function call in `addConnection()` function for simplicity; in real-world applications, it would be better to get the time from a system timer asynchronously using another thread or signal handling mechanism instead of calling `time()` every time a new connection is added.

Lastly, for scalability and high load conditions, you may consider implementing a distributed hash table across multiple servers or utilize consistent hashing for even distribution of network interfaces across nodes in the cluster. Also, it would be wise to use a more advanced data structure for the connections list depending on the specifics of your project (for example, binary search trees if connection IDs are sorted). The performance can also be improved by using a faster hash function or employing a different collision resolution strategy. But these considerations will require significant changes and might not fit in this simple implementation.
