["Here is a C# code block that accomplishes this task using Dapper micro-ORM to simplify database operations. Please note that proper error handling and exception management have been omitted for brevity, but should be incorporated into actual implementations.

```C#
using System;
using System.Data;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using Dapper;

public class SqlQueryRunner : IDisposable
{
    private readonly IDbConnection _connection;
    private readonly ICacheService _cacheService; // Assumes an interface for caching service

    public SqlQueryRunner(string connectionString, ICacheService cacheService)
    {
        _connection = new NpgsqlConnection(connectionString);
        _cacheService = cacheService;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _connection?.Dispose();
    }

    public IDataReader ExecuteQuery(string query, DynamicParameters parameters)
    {
        if (_cacheService.TryGetValue(query, out var cachedResult))
        {
            return new DataTableReader(cachedResult);
        }

        using (var multi = _connection.QueryMultiple(query, parameters))
        {
            var resultSet = multi.Read();

            if (_cacheService.IsCachingEnabled)
            {
                // Cache the results for future use with the same query
                _cacheService.AddOrUpdate(query, resultSet);
            }

            return new DataTableReader(resultSet);
        }
    }
}
```
This class has a connection to the database and a cache service (assumed to be an interface for caching). It provides a method `ExecuteQuery` that checks if the query result is cached, returning the cached value if it exists. If not, it executes the SQL command using Dapper's QueryMultiple function, retrieves the results and caches them if required.

This solution assumes usage of Dapper for simplifying database operations, but other ORM libraries like Entity Framework can be used as well. Also, an example implementation of the `ICacheService` is not provided here, which should handle how cached values are stored, retrieved and invalidated based on schema changes.
