["Implementing an Octree data structure from scratch in C++ is a complex task that involves many different aspects of software engineering and computer science. Here's a simplified version of how you might create the data structure and some basic functions to interact with it. This won't include all features you requested, but this will give you a starting point. Also, note that writing a garbage collector in C++ is an advanced topic and beyond the scope of this response.

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>
#include <cassert>
#include <algorithm>

struct Node {
    std::mutex mtx; // for thread safety 

    int data;
    std::vector<Node*> children;
};

class Octree {
private:
    Node* root = nullptr;

public:
    Octree() {}

    void insert(int value, const std::string& path) {
        if (!root) { // if the tree is empty 
            root = new Node{value};
            return;
        }

        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(root->mtx);
        insertHelper(root, value, path, 0);
    }

    void insertHelper(Node* node, int value, const std::string& path, size_t index) {
        if (index == path.size()) { // end of the path reached
            node->data = value;
            return;
        }

        char direction = path[index];
        assert(direction >= '0' && direction <= '7');

        Node* child = nullptr;
        if (node->children.size() > direction - '0') { // the child already exists 
            child = node->children[direction - '0'];
        } else { // create a new child 
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(root->mtx); // to ensure thread safety

            if (node->children.size() <= direction - '0') {
                node->children.resize(direction - '0' + 1, nullptr);
            }

            child = new Node;
            node->children[direction - '0'] = child;
        }

        insertHelper(child, value, path, index+1); // recursive call 
    }

    int retrieve(const std::string& path) {
        if (!root) {
            return 0; // assuming zero is neutral element for your data
        }

        Node* current = root;
        for (char direction : path) {
            assert(direction >= '0' && direction <= '7');

            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(current->mtx);
            if (!current->children[direction - '0']) { // node doesn't exist 
                return 0;
            }

            current = current->children[direction - '0']; // move to the next node
        }

        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(current->mtx);
        return current->data;
    }
};
```
The code above is a simplified version of an Octree where each node can have 8 children (represented as '0' to '7'). The `insert` method creates nodes along the path if they don't exist. It also has thread safety measures in place with mutex locks to avoid race conditions.
The `retrieve` function is similar, but it only returns data when a full path exists and doesn't create new nodes.

Please note that this is just a starting point. The implementation of an Octree is complex and involves many aspects such as dynamic resizing, memory management, custom garbage collection, cache optimizations and so on which are beyond the scope of this response due to their complexity. You should study and apply appropriate design patterns, strategies and best practices in your specific use case.
"]

