["This task requires extensive coding which involves knowledge of C programming language, high-level design principles and understanding of concurrent systems. Here is a sample code that starts with some basic structure for your request. Please note that actual implementation would involve many more details including error handling, memory management, thread synchronization etc., which are not covered here due to the complexity:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct connection {
    int socket_descriptor;
    char remote_ip[16];
    int port_number;
} Connection;

typedef struct state {
    Connection* conn;
    struct state** next_states; // Array of pointers to next states
} State;

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
State** visitedStates; 

Connection* create_connection(int socket, char* ip, int port) {
    Connection* newConn = (Connection*) malloc(sizeof(Connection));
    if (!newConn) return NULL;

    newConn->socket_descriptor = socket;
    strncpy(newConn->remote_ip, ip, sizeof(newConn->remote_ip) - 1);
    newConn->port_number = port;

    return newConn;
}

void add_state(State** currentStates, State* newState) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    // Add to the list of visited states
    visitedStates = (State**) realloc(visitedStates, sizeof(*visitedStates)*((currentStates - &newState) + 1));
    visitedStates[currentStates - &newState] = newState; 

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

int isVisited(State* state){
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(visitedStates)/sizeof(*visitedStates); ++i) {
        if (state == visitedStates[i]) return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}

State** get_next_states(State* state, int depth){
    State** allStates = NULL; 

    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(state->next_states)/sizeof(*state->next_states); ++i) {
        if (!isVisited(state)) add_state(&allStates, state); 

        // Recurse only if the depth limit has not been reached and it is not visited
        if (depth > 0 && !isVisited(state->next_states[i])) {
            get_next_states(state->next_states[i], --depth); 
        }
    }

    return allStates;
}
```
In the above code, we first create a connection record with details like socket descriptor, remote IP address and port number. This is stored in an array for management purposes. We then define states within our finite state automaton (FSA). Each state contains a reference to its associated connection as well as references to other possible next states it can transition into.

We use a mutex lock for thread safety, where only one thread at a time is allowed to add a state to the visited list and retrieve all reachable states in the FSA. This ensures that the system remains synchronized even when called concurrently. We also include mechanisms for checking if a state has been visited before to avoid cycles during traversal.

This code doesn't cover complete functionalities but should give you an idea about how such a function could start to look like. For memory management, efficient data retrieval from external sources, adaptive timeout logic and other features you might need more complex implementations or even use of pre-existing libraries/frameworks which have already tackled these problems.
"]

