["Here is a basic thread-safe queue manager in C. It uses a linked list as its underlying data structure for flexibility and to minimize memory fragmentation. The linked list nodes are dynamically allocated, so that the manager can be used with any type of data. 

This implementation includes error handling mechanisms to gracefully handle external data source failures, and it also employs synchronization primitives such as mutexes and condition variables for thread safety. However, keep in mind that this is a basic example and does not include all the design challenges or trade-offs you mentioned. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef struct Node {
    void *data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

typedef struct QueueManager {
    Node *head;
    Node *tail;
    pthread_mutex_t headLock;
    pthread_mutex_t tailLock;
    pthread_cond_t cond;
} QueueManager;

void enqueue(QueueManager *qm, void *data) {
    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        errno = ENOMEM; // Out of memory error.
        return;
    }

    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = NULL;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&qm->tailLock);
    if (qm->tail) {
        qm->tail->next = newNode;
        qm->tail = newNode;
    } else {
        qm->head = newNode;
        qm->tail = newNode;
    }

    pthread_cond_signal(&qm->cond); // Wake up dequeue waiting thread.
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&qm->tailLock); 
}

void *dequeue(QueueManager *qm) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&qm->headLock);

    while (qm->head == NULL) { // If queue is empty.
        pthread_cond_wait(&qm->cond, &qm->headLock); // Wait until a new item is added.
    }

    Node *oldHead = qm->head;
    void *data = oldHead->data;

    if (qm->tail == qm->head) { 
        // If there's only one node in queue, set tail to NULL.
        qm->tail = NULL; 
    }

    qm->head = oldHead->next;

    free(oldHead); // Free memory of dequeued node.
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&qm->headLock);

    return data;
}
```

This code can be enhanced further to include more design challenges and trade-offs as per the requirements. However, doing so might complicate the code without providing significant performance or memory improvements. 

It should be noted that while the queue manager provided here is thread-safe, it doesn't manage its own memory; any data enqueued into the queue must be dynamically allocated and manually deallocated by the calling code when no longer needed to avoid memory leaks.
"]

