['Below is an example implementation of the `StringHasher` class according to your specifications:

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class StringHasher {
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 10000;

    private Map<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private LinkedList<String> lruQueue = new LinkedList<>();
    private Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(StringHasher.class.getName());

    private MessageDigest md5;

    public StringHasher() {
        try {
            md5 = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            logger.log(Level.SEVERE, "MD5 algorithm not supported", e);
        }
    }

    public String hashString(String input) throws NoSuchElementException {
        if (cache.containsKey(input)) {
            // Move the key to the front of LRU queue
            lruQueue.remove(input);
            lruQueue.addFirst(input);

            return cache.get(input);
        } else {
            byte[] messageDigest = md5.digest(input.getBytes());
            String hashedString = new String(messageDigest);

            if (lruQueue.size() == MAX_CACHE_SIZE) {
                // Remove the least recently used entry from cache and queue
                String lruKey = lruQueue.removeLast();
                cache.remove(lruKey);
            }

            // Add new entry to cache and front of LRU queue
            cache.put(input, hashedString);
            lruQueue.addFirst(input);

            return hashedString;
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that this is a simple implementation for demonstration purposes only. It lacks the following features:
- Configuration options, logging level configuration, and integration with a logging framework
