['Here is a Java solution that uses ConcurrentHashMap to manage multiple concurrent characters with unique cooldown periods. It also uses AtomicInteger to generate unique identifiers for each attack. The method selectAttackSequence uses FutureTask to asynchronously fetch the attack sequence from an external data source, handling network failures by retrying within a specified timeout period.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.math.*;
import java.nio.charset.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class GameCharacter {
    private static final int ATTACK_COUNT = 2;
    private static final long COOLDOWN = TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(9);

    // ConcurrentHashMap to manage multiple concurrent characters with unique cooldown periods
    private static Map<String, Long> characterCooldowns = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    // AtomicInteger to generate unique identifiers for each attack
    private static AtomicInteger attackCounter = new AtomicInteger(1);

    public String selectAttackSequence(String characterName) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        long cooldownStartTime;

        // Compute the MD5 hash of the character's name
        MessageDigest md;
        try {
            md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(characterName.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            BigInteger number = new BigInteger(1, hashBytes);
            String hashText = number.toString(16);

            // Update character's cooldown period
            while ((cooldownStartTime = System.currentTimeMillis() - characterCooldowns.computeIfAbsent(hashText, k -> 0L)) < COOLDOWN) {
                Thread.sleep(COOLDOWN - cooldownStartTime);
            }

            // Randomly select the next attack number
            int selectedAttack = new Random().nextInt(ATTACK_COUNT) + 1;
            // Make sure the selection is not the same as the previous one
            if (selectedAttack == characterCooldowns.getOrDefault(hashText, 0)) {
                selectedAttack = ATTACK_COUNT - selectedAttack + 1;
            }

            System.out.println("Selected attack number: " + selectedAttack);

            // Update the cooldown start time for the character
            characterCooldowns.put(hashText, System.currentTimeMillis());

            return Integer.toString(selectedAttack);
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("MD5 hash not available!");
        }
    }
}
```

Note: This implementation assumes that the attack selection process can be performed efficiently, without blocking the characterCooldowns map. If this is not feasible, other concurrency control mechanisms such as locks or synchronized blocks may need to be used instead. The cooldown period is implemented by sleeping until the cooldown period has passed before updating the cooldown start time for the character. This assumes that the duration of the attack selection process can be neglected compared to the cooldown period, which may not always be true in a real-world scenario.
']

