['Here's a simple implementation of MD5 hash generation utility class with concurrency, caching, logging, configurable expiration policy, exception handling mechanism and synchronization issues handling using `java.util.concurrent` and Google Guava libraries. 

```Java
import com.google.common.cache.*;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class MD5HashGenerator {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MD5HashGenerator.class);
    private LoadingCache<String, String> cache;

    public MD5HashGenerator() {
        this.cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(10_000) // maximum size based on requirements
                .expireAfterWrite(24, TimeUnit.HOURS) // expiration policy based on requirements
                .build(new CacheLoader<String, String>() { 
                    @Override
                    public String load(String key) throws Exception {
                        return generateMD5Hash(key);
                    }
                 });
    }

    public String getHash(String input) {
        try {
            long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            // Retrieve from cache or compute if not cached
            String hash = this.cache.get(input);

            logger.info("MD5 Hash generation time for '{}': {}ms", input, (System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime));
            return hash;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exception gracefully
            logger.error("Error while generating MD5 Hash", e);
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }

    private String generateMD5Hash(String input) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        return bytesToHex(hashBytes);
    }

    private String bytesToHex(byte[] bytes) {
        StringBuilder hexString = new StringBuilder();

        for (byte b : bytes) {
            hexString.append(String.format("%02X", 0xFF & b));
        }

        return hexString.toString();
    }
}
```
This implementation uses Google Guava's LoadingCache to automatically compute and store the MD5 hash of a given input string when it is not in cache, or retrieve it from cache if it has been already computed. Cache expiration policy could be adjusted according to your requirements (e.g., setting an absolute expiration time, or using soft references).

Please note that this is a simplified version. In production environment, you might want to have more sophisticated exception handling mechanism and logging strategy. You should also consider using stronger hashing algorithms like SHA-256 for secure applications.
