['Here is a basic C# class named `DynamicValueGenerator` fulfilling all the mentioned criteria:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

public class DynamicValueGenerator
{
    private float minValue, maxValue;
    private Random rand = new Random();

    public DynamicValueGenerator(float minValue, float maxValue)
    {
        if (minValue > maxValue)
            throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value should be less than or equal to maximum value.");

        this.minValue = minValue;
        this.maxValue = maxValue + 1f; // To ensure range is inclusive of upper bound for integers
    }

    public float GetRandomFloat() => (float)(minValue + rand.NextDouble() * (maxValue - minValue));

    public int GetRandomIntExclusiveUpper() => rand.Next(Convert.ToInt32(Math.Ceiling(minValue)), Convert.ToInt32(Math.Floor(maxValue)));

    public int GetRandomIntExclusiveLower() => rand.Next(Convert.ToInt32(Math.Ceiling(minValue)) + 1, Convert.ToInt32(Math.Floor(maxValue)));

    public override string ToString() => $"Range: [{minValue}, {maxValue})";
}
``` 

The `GetRandomFloat` and `GetRandomIntExclusiveUpper` methods use C#'s Random class which should be thread-safe in most cases. 

Note that the range for integers is inclusive of both ends, as per your request. However, due to rounding issues with floating-point values when minValue == maxValue and using a random number generator to select an integer from this range may lead to undefined behavior if the Random object is not properly seeded or reused in other parts of the code.

