['Here is a simple C code implementing the task using a linked list to store `NetworkConnectionStats` structures and pthreads for handling multiple threads safely. Please note that full implementation including error handling and concurrency management may be complex depending on specific requirements, so this example only covers basic operations and does not include some real-world considerations such as network delays or failure scenarios:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct NetworkConnectionStats {
    int connectionID;
    time_t timestamp;
    double throughputData;
    struct NetworkConnectionStats *next;
} NetworkConnectionStats;

NetworkConnectionStats* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void addEntry(int connectionID, double throughputData) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    NetworkConnectionStats *newNode = malloc(sizeof(NetworkConnectionStats));
    newNode->connectionID = connectionID;
    newNode->timestamp = time(NULL);
    newNode->throughputData = throughputData;
    newNode->next = head;

    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

NetworkConnectionStats* getEntry(int connectionID) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    NetworkConnectionStats *current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        if (current->connectionID == connectionID) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return current;
        }
        current = current->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;  // Connection not found
}

void cleanupEntries(int maxAgeSeconds) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    time_t expiryTime = time(NULL) - maxAgeSeconds;
    while (head != NULL && head->timestamp < expiryTime) {
        NetworkConnectionStats *oldHead = head;
        head = head->next;

        free(oldHead);  // Cleanup old entry
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This implementation assumes that the data sources for `NetworkConnectionStats` structures are reliable and will not require exception handling. For real-world scenarios, you might need to use more robust error handling or retry mechanisms. This simple code also does not handle concurrent access from multiple threads or processes well - it would be better to have a lock per connection to avoid race conditions between threads, or use other synchronization primitives such as condition variables or atomic operations.
